{
  "$comment": [
    "Deep Frontier toolbar sub-groups (Moddable Tool Groups 11.0.0, a required mod). Written by tools/tool_group_content.mjs at the end of canonical generation; checked by tools/validate_tool_groups.mjs. Contracts below are source-confirmed in ConfigurableToolGroups.dll (ModdableToolGroupSpecService, CustomBlockObjectButtons).",
    "Each group is a BlockObjectToolGroupSpec with ParentToolGroupSpec.ParentIds [parent]: a button inside that group. parent is a native tool group or another group declared here. order orders it among the parent's buttons on the same scale as tool ToolOrder (vanilla tools use about 10-100). name is its English name, icon the template whose LabeledEntitySpec icon it shows (a tool in the group or below it).",
    "templates: Deep Frontier buildings (template name without the faction suffix); the generator sets their PlaceableBlockObjectSpec.ToolGroupId to the group.",
    "claims: other templates (vanilla, a required mod, or an optional mod from externalMods), by full template name. They go in the group's ToolGroupChildrenSpec.ChildrenToolsTemplateNames with DoNotIncludePlaceableToolGroup, which takes them out of their own group; their blueprints are not touched. A claimed template that is not loaded is skipped without a warning, and a group with no loaded tool is never created, so a group of an absent optional mod's tools does not appear.",
    "A group with claims lists its tools in ChildrenOrderedIds: claims in the order given, then templates. Otherwise templates keep their ToolOrder. A group holds either tools or sub-groups, not both.",
    "Left to right is the unlock order (user 2026-11-01; tools/validate_tool_order.mjs): inside a Deep Frontier group every tool, claims included, follows science ascending (ties: a tool whose products another tool consumes comes first, then the smaller footprint, then the lower one); a group's order puts it among its parent's buttons by its earliest unlock, at a place with the fewest science inversions against the vanilla and other-mod buttons there, whose own order is kept. Deep Frontier tools placed in a native group follow the same rule.",
    "upgrades: a Deep Frontier building that upgrades another, by template name without the faction suffix -> the full template name of the building it upgrades (user 2026-11-02: the Reactor Charging Bay right next to the Charging Station). Its producer puts it in the base building's tool group with an order that stands it immediately to the base's right, and it is not listed in any group here. tools/validate_tool_order.mjs requires that adjacency and leaves the upgrade out of the science-order rules (its unlock still comes from its science); every other tool keeps them.",
    "nativeOrders: the complete order of a native group's tools, written as a same-path partial of that group's blueprint, {ToolGroupChildrenSpec: {ChildrenOrderedIds#append: [...]}} (ids get 10, 20, ...; #append keeps other mods' entries).",
    "related: retained design context for associated tools and sub-groups. Ordering follows the science-order rules; these associations no longer impose adjacency or exempt children from those rules. Only declared upgrades require immediate adjacency to their base building.",
    "externalMods: the non-Deep Frontier, non-vanilla templates claimed, ordered or kept together here, with the installed package they were read from, and each template's tool group, science (BuildingSpec.ScienceCost), ToolOrder (the menu order of an unclaimed one) and icon. optional mods are not in the manifest's RequiredMods. Flywheels keeps its blueprints in its asset bundle (read with UnityPy from AssetBundles/flywheels_win at 4.1.1.15): its template names swap Horizontal and Vertical against the display names, so the groups follow the display names."
  ],
